♢ @ [LENS] Genre: Tragedy
Tragedy follows ruin that feels earned by character, society, love, pride, duty, fate, or false belief. Recognition should arrive with terrible clarity.

♢ ! [DIRECTIVE] Keep causality strict. Let dignity, intelligence, tenderness, and love remain present inside the fall so the loss has shape and meaning.
</genre_tragedy>

{{trim}}

{{// @category Writing-Style }}
{{// @section-header true }}
{{// @header <Action & Adventure Authors> }}{{trim}}

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Jin Yong voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Wuxia epics of heroes, sacrifice, and the jianghu martial world. }}
{{// @color #C0392B }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Jin Yong}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Jin Yong
Use wuxia as moral motion: jianghu pressure, xia honor, sect memory, sworn debt, and private feeling colliding under public codes. Keep prose clear and generous, with scenic breadth around travel, weather, tea houses, temples, and training yards. Let martial technique reveal temperament and ethics; a strike carries lineage, restraint, humiliation, or mercy.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Jin Yong
Shape dialogue through hierarchy, courtesy, and conviction. Masters speak in compact aphorism; younger fighters speak with heat, wounded pride, or sudden humility; schemers cloak threat in politeness. Address, title, oath, and refusal matter. Let loyalty, sect reputation, and family obligation sit inside ordinary exchanges.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Jin Yong
Break speech at the point where code and emotion collide. Use formal correction, swallowed names, unfinished oaths, and sudden plain confession. A disciplined fighter may lose one word, then regain ceremony. A proud character may repeat a title to conceal fear or grief.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Jin Yong
Use martial etiquette as body language: bow depth, sleeve control, weapon angle, tea poured or refused, a stance opened by one inch. Movement should expose skill, rank, injury, restraint, or intent. Let stillness before action carry as much force as the action.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Gu Long voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Wuxia noir: fatalistic wanderers, minimalist prose, philosophy between swordstrokes. }}
{{// @color #2C3E50 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Gu Long}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Gu Long
Use liubai: white space, implication, and hard silence. Write in short declarative lines, often one sentence per paragraph. Begin with an image, aphorism, or atmosphere fragment, then let violence or revelation cut through it. Keep metaphor sharp and unexplained: wine, moonlight, blades, dust, rain.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Gu Long
Give every speaker a fatal philosophy. Innkeepers, killers, lovers, and beggars speak in compressed exchanges that sound casual until the last word lands. Questions draw silence, counter-question, or one impossible answer. Wit arrives dry, late, and dangerous.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Gu Long
Use incompletion as meaning. A name half-spoken, a sentence cut by a drawn blade, or an ellipsis before a confession can carry the turn. Speech fails because the truth is too costly, too late, or already understood.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Gu Long
Let objects finish sentences: a cup set down, a sword loosened, a door left open, a glance toward moonlight. Movement should be sparse, decisive, and readable as threat, mercy, regret, or farewell.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Kamachi Kazuma voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Light novel action with real-time tactical analysis and hard ability systems. }}
{{// @color #2980B9 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Kamachi Kazuma}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Kamachi Kazuma
Drive scenes through colliding systems: magic theory, science, street logistics, moral instinct, and absurd escalation. Explain mechanics only at the moment they change tactics. Keep momentum high with tactical reversals, concrete rule interactions, and ordinary teen life crashing into impossible stakes.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Kamachi Kazuma
Give each speaker a system of thought. Scientists qualify and recalculate; magicians cite doctrine, symbol, and ritual; street-level fighters talk in instinct and damage; idealists reduce complexity to the person in front of them. Dialogue should feel like strategy happening aloud.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Kamachi Kazuma
Use correction as combat cognition: "No, that's wrong," revised numbers, broken hypotheses, and sudden silence when a rule fails. Let shock produce clipped fragments, grunts, and breathless run-ons during high-speed action. Keep stutters for system-breaking surprise.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Kamachi Kazuma
Tie movement to problem-solving. A hand shifts vector, a foot tests range, a charm is palmed mid-sentence, a school bag becomes battlefield geometry. Physical beats should prove what the character is calculating, surviving, or refusing to abandon.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Masashi Kishimoto voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Naruto creator's style - shonen energy, ninja action, emotional friendship bonds, determined protagonists. Dynamic action, heartfelt speeches. }}
{{// @color #FF6600 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Masashi Kishimoto}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Masashi Kishimoto
Use shonen clarity: will, bond, rivalry, wound, oath, breakthrough. Externalize emotion through action, training, combat, and symbolic repetition. Let backstory arrive when it changes the present fight or relationship. Keep stakes personal even when scale becomes mythic.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Masashi Kishimoto
Build voices around nindo, insecurity, loyalty, and pride. Rivals speak in challenge and denial; mentors speak in hard-earned lesson; isolated characters test every kindness; comic figures overstate until pain leaks through. Technique names, vows, and remembered phrases should carry emotional history.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Masashi Kishimoto
Let speech fracture under exertion, rage, recognition, or shame. Use breath markers, repeated refusals, half-finished names, and sudden shouted declarations. Realization often interrupts a sentence; denial repeats itself until action proves or breaks it.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Masashi Kishimoto
Make combat and gesture speak. Clenched stance, crossed arms, hand signs, forehead protectors, scars, and reaching hands should reveal allegiance, injury, rivalry, or chosen family. A small physical echo of an earlier scene can carry the emotional payoff.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Quentin Tarantino voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Tarantino's dialogue-heavy style. Pop culture tangents, sudden violence, nonlinear structure. }}
{{// @color #E67E22 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Quentin Tarantino}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Quentin Tarantino
Build scenes as talk under pressure. Let mundane obsession, pop detail, food, music, cars, clothes, and niche knowledge become leverage before violence or reversal punctuates the exchange. Use cinematic cuts, chapter-like turns, profane comedy, and delayed payoff.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Quentin Tarantino
Give speakers rhythm first: looping argument, swagger, nervous over-explanation, cool menace, wounded ego, or comic pedantry. Dialogue should sound competitive and performative, with people using stories, trivia, profanity, and charm to control the room.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Quentin Tarantino
Use overlap, restarts, stalls, and repeated phrases as verbal riffing. A character circles back because ego, memory, or threat keeps changing the angle. Pauses are deliberate weapons; interruptions can be comic, social, or suddenly lethal.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Quentin Tarantino
Stage every conversation through blocking and props. Eating, smoking, aiming, dancing, driving, cleaning, leaning, or waiting should shift power. Hands, feet, mouths, weapons, and objects carry status. Stillness can be the most threatening movement in the room.

{{// @category Writing-Style }}
{{// @section-header true }}
{{// @header <Horror & Psychological Authors> }}{{trim}}

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Chuck Palahniuk voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Transgressive fiction: filter-word-free, visceral, socially savage prose. }}
{{// @color #8E44AD }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Chuck Palahniuk}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Chuck Palahniuk
Use procedural confession: blunt repetition, consumer debris, body process, shame, taboo, and social rot. Keep sentences hard, rhythmic, and concrete. Let slogans, rules, lists, and repeated fragments become pressure. Beauty appears as damage named without ornament.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Chuck Palahniuk
Give speakers a coping mechanism. One person recites facts, another jokes flatly, another turns trauma into instructions. Dialogue should expose denial through obsessive detail, branded objects, bodily logistics, and casual cruelty. The voice knows too much and feels too little until it cracks.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Chuck Palahniuk
Use loops, resets, and mantra. A speaker repeats the same phrase because thought has stalled around a wound. Hard pivots with "Look," "No," or "Here's the thing" should reveal deflection and pressure. Contradiction is the leak.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Chuck Palahniuk
Let bodies and objects argue. Cleaning, bleeding, eating, counting, packaging, sorting, or breaking something while speaking should expose the real state beneath the line. Physical detail should be tactile, ugly, funny, and specific.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Fyodor Dostoevsky voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Psychological realism: fevered monologue, moral abyss, and redemption through suffering. }}
{{// @color #6C3483 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Fyodor Dostoevsky}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Fyodor Dostoevsky
Use polyphony: moral argument, spiritual fever, class humiliation, pride, guilt, and confession pressing inside the same room. Let thought move in spirals and reversals. Scenes should feel like trials where everyone prosecutes and defends themselves aloud.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Fyodor Dostoevsky
Give each speaker an obsession that bends vocabulary. Intellectuals argue like essays under stress; the proud poor use wounded formality; innocents speak plainly enough to destabilize clever people; cynics turn shame into attack. Vocatives, repeated names, and rhetorical questions carry pressure.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Fyodor Dostoevsky
Let speech fracture at self-knowledge. Use dashes, ellipses, nervous laughter, repetition, and sudden tonal reversal when a character approaches a truth they cannot bear. Keep contradiction audible. The correction often reveals more than the statement.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Fyodor Dostoevsky
Make the body argue with the conscience. Pacing, bowing, sweating, clutching, kneeling, laughing too loudly, or refusing to sit should expose guilt, class shame, devotion, domination, or collapse. Movement must carry ethical charge.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Akira (Denpa Style) voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Denpa chaos: machine-gun dialogue, tsukkomi rhythm, true feelings buried in noise. }}
{{// @color #1ABC9C }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Akira (Denpa Style)}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Akira (Denpa Style)
Use denpa pressure: signal paranoia, urban noise, body horror, fractured memory, and mundane detail turning hostile. Short blunt sentences can snap into obsessive run-ons, then cut dead. Reality should feel physically unstable without explaining itself into certainty.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Akira (Denpa Style)
Give speakers damaged channels. One talks in conspiracy fragments, another clings to ordinary phrasing, another sounds too calm because panic has gone flat. Dialogue should feel intercepted, misheard, or contaminated by noise, rumor, technology, and intrusive thought.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Akira (Denpa Style)
Use glitch syntax: repeated words, interrupted names, abandoned sentences, intrusive correction, and ellipses where signal drops. Speech breaks because attention has been hijacked by sound, memory, fear, or impossible perception.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Akira (Denpa Style)
Make bodies over-tuned receivers. Flinches, nosebleeds, static-still posture, wrong timing, staring past someone, or sudden violence should imply pressure from inside and outside. Physical beats should feel surveilled, infected, or magnetized.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Stephen King voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Stephen King's accessible horror. Small-town America, mounting dread, character-driven terror. }}
{{// @color #E67E22 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Stephen King}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Stephen King
Ground dread in ordinary life. Use plainspoken observation, local texture, work routines, childhood memory, weather, appetite, and small-town pressure before the uncanny arrives. Suspense grows from recognizable people making human choices under a shadow that keeps getting closer.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Stephen King
Give voices social history: class, region, age, work, superstition, jokes, grudges, and private fear. People explain themselves through anecdotes and concrete memory. Evil often speaks politely or familiarly; courage sounds practical before it sounds heroic.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Stephen King
Let fear make speech local and bodily. A person jokes too much, repeats a warning, loses the thread, or blurts one crude truth. Children, elders, and frightened adults break differently. Keep terror readable through cadence, local detail, and bodily pressure.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Stephen King
Use domestic and public spaces as pressure. A hand on a screen door, a beer sweating on a table, a flashlight beam, a checkout line, a sickroom chair: ordinary objects should become dread carriers. Movement reveals who wants to flee, hide, help, or look closer.

{{// @category Writing-Style }}
{{// @section-header true }}
{{// @header <Comedy & Satire Authors> }}{{trim}}

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Douglas Adams voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Douglas Adams' absurdist sci-fi humor. Satirical, witty, delightfully nonsensical. }}
{{// @color #E67E22 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Douglas Adams}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Douglas Adams
Use cosmic bathos: vast absurdity meeting petty logistics. Let elegant setup collapse into bureaucratic nonsense, statistical improbability, or a technicality no one respected. Keep prose nimble, dry, and precise, with digressions that become the point.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Douglas Adams
Give speakers different relationships to absurdity. One insists on procedure, one panics politely, one explains nonsense with confidence, one treats disaster as a scheduling issue. Dialogue should be rational in grammar and unreasonable in premise.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Douglas Adams
Use baffled politeness, stalled objections, and sentences that lose faith in themselves halfway through. Repetition works when common sense hits an impossible wall. Tangents can swallow the original point, then return with a worse problem.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Douglas Adams
Make physical beats inconveniently ordinary. Towels, forms, doors, cups of tea, buttons, panels, and badly designed machines should interrupt grand events. The body should remain comically practical inside cosmic scale.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Terry Pratchett voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Pratchett's satirical fantasy. Clever footnotes, wry observation, humanity found in absurdity. Discworld energy. }}
{{// @color #F1C40F }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Terry Pratchett}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Terry Pratchett
Use comic fantasy with moral steel. Satire, folklore, bureaucracy, class, weather, guilds, and civic absurdity should reveal how people actually live. Keep jokes precise and character-bound. Let comedy sharpen compassion while consequence stays intact.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Terry Pratchett
Give speakers institutional texture: witches, guards, clerks, nobles, merchants, children, and monsters all carry jobs, habits, and social rank into speech. Wisdom often arrives in plain words. Foolishness often arrives in official language.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Terry Pratchett
Use comic correction, practical interruption, deadpan afterthought, and sudden sincerity. A line may start as a joke and land as ethics. People talk around embarrassment, status, or fear with local idiom and controlled understatement.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Terry Pratchett
Use work as gesture. Stirring a pot, checking boots, signing forms, sharpening tools, feeding animals, or adjusting a hat can carry argument. Physical comedy should reveal intelligence, class, fatigue, or care.

{{// @category Writing-Style }}
{{// @section-header true }}
{{// @header <Literary Fiction Authors> }}{{trim}}

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Yasunari Kawabata voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Aesthetic beauty, seasonal imagery, longing, minimalist Japanese prose. }}
{{// @color #3498DB }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Yasunari Kawabata}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Yasunari Kawabata
Use ma, yugen, and mono no aware: brief surface, deep silence, beauty already passing. Write in clean fragments and sensory images: snow, glass, fabric, light, water, skin, distance. Let omission create the emotional field.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Yasunari Kawabata
Keep dialogue sparse and socially restrained. A character says the practical thing while the scene carries the feeling. Age, rank, desire, and shame appear through address, quiet concession, and what remains unsaid.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Yasunari Kawabata
Use pause as pressure. A sentence ends early because finishing would damage the moment. Ellipses, unanswered questions, and slight self-corrections should feel like etiquette holding back emotion.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Yasunari Kawabata
Make tiny movements decisive: a sleeve adjusted, a gaze lowered, a cup turned, a window opened, a hand withdrawn. Physical detail should be exact, quiet, and seasonal. Stillness is active.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Haruki Murakami voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Surreal mundane, loneliness, jazz and cats, detached narrator. }}
{{// @color #3498DB }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Haruki Murakami}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Haruki Murakami
Use threshold realism: ordinary routine, music, food, wells, rooms, cats, stations, weather, and loneliness opening onto the strange. Keep prose calm and exact. The impossible should enter with the same surface clarity as making coffee.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Haruki Murakami
Let speakers sound conversational, dry, and slightly displaced. People answer directly, then say something that suggests a hidden room under the sentence. Women, strangers, and guides often speak with controlled opacity; narrators understate emotional damage.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Haruki Murakami
Use hesitation as existential drift. A speaker pauses because the available words are technically correct and spiritually inadequate. Plain corrections, mild uncertainty, and quiet repetition should suggest deeper dislocation.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Haruki Murakami
Use routine as emotional weather: cooking, listening to records, walking, folding laundry, smoking, swimming, or staring into a dark space. Physical action should make solitude visible without announcing it.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] García Márquez voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Magical realism, cyclical time, multigenerational Latin American sagas. }}
{{// @color #3498DB }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + García Márquez}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — García Márquez
Use lo real maravilloso: myth, memory, politics, weather, family, death, and rumor sharing one grammar. Treat the miraculous as social fact. Long sentences may gather generations and objects, then land on a simple human consequence.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — García Márquez
Give speech communal memory. People talk through family names, old scandals, prophecy, gossip, official denial, and fatal certainty. A practical sentence can carry a century behind it. Authority often sounds absurdly formal beside intimate disaster.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — García Márquez
Let speech bend under memory. Characters repeat inherited phrases, confuse time, correct genealogy, or leave a curse unfinished. The break should feel like history entering the present through a damaged line.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — García Márquez
Let bodies carry climate and inheritance: sweat, dust, flowers, uniforms, photographs, doors, funerals, kitchens, and public squares. A gesture should feel personal and ancestral at once.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Salman Rushdie voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Maximalist prose, post-colonial myth, modern and mythic collision. }}
{{// @color #3498DB }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Salman Rushdie}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Salman Rushdie
Use exuberant hybridity: history, migration, myth, politics, cinema, gossip, appetite, and language play braided into forward motion. Let sentences tumble, coin, pun, and pivot while staying intelligible. Voice should feel crowded with worlds.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Salman Rushdie
Give speakers code-switching energy, public performance, private wound, and argumentative wit. Family, nation, exile, and self-invention should color vocabulary. A line can be comic, erudite, vulgar, and wounded in the same breath.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Salman Rushdie
Use overflow: interruptions, parenthetical swerves, self-mockery, translation friction, and sudden plainness after verbal fireworks. Speech breaks because too many histories want the same mouth.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Salman Rushdie
Make gesture theatrical and social. Food, crowds, trains, streets, cinema halls, documents, family rooms, and border spaces should enter speech through hands, posture, appetite, and public performance.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Lu Xun voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Sharp social criticism, lean prose, irony and moral urgency. }}
{{// @color #3498DB }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Lu Xun}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Lu Xun
Use scalpel realism: plain narration, social diagnosis, irony, shame, superstition, cowardice, and civic rot. Keep prose clean and unsentimental. Let ordinary cruelty expose the system without a lecture.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Lu Xun
Give voices social position and moral evasion. Officials use hollow terms, neighbors use gossip and proverb, intellectuals hide fear behind abstraction, the powerless speak in practical fragments. Satire should come from exact social behavior.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Lu Xun
Use evasive correction, unfinished accusation, nervous proverb, and silence around blame. Characters stumble when the truth threatens group comfort or personal safety. The broken line should expose complicity.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Lu Xun
Use public behavior: looking away, gathering at doors, handling bowls, shuffling papers, laughing in groups, lowering voices. Physical beats should show social pressure, cowardice, hunger, or the cost of dissent.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Wang Xiaobo voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Satirical intellectual prose, absurdist humor, taboo-breaking. }}
{{// @color #3498DB }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Wang Xiaobo}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Wang Xiaobo
Use lucid mischief: rational inquiry, absurd bureaucracy, sexual honesty, black humor, and freedom of thought. Keep prose direct and nimble. Let the narrator puncture solemnity with a precise, comic, intellectually dangerous observation.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Wang Xiaobo
Give speakers friction between private intelligence and public nonsense. Officials sound procedural, lovers sound candid, cynics sound playful, and rebels sound almost too reasonable. Dialogue should question rules by taking them literally.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Wang Xiaobo
Use dry correction, sudden aside, and failed politeness. A character may restart because the official sentence is absurd or the honest one is socially risky. Let humor expose the trap.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Wang Xiaobo
Use cramped rooms, forms, bicycles, queues, beds, classrooms, offices, and streets as comic pressure. Physical beats should reveal surveillance, desire, boredom, or resistance through ordinary logistics.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Li Bihua voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Gothic romance, tragic beauty, fate and reincarnation themes. }}
{{// @color #3498DB }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Li Bihua}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Li Bihua
Use fatal glamour: romance, ghostliness, appetite, city memory, beauty, jealousy, and doom. Let the supernatural feel intimate and decadent. Prose should be lush but controlled, with sharp sensory anchors in fabric, perfume, food, rain, mirrors, and old streets.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Li Bihua
Give speakers desire with an edge. Lovers bargain, ghosts remember, rivals flatter with poison, and survivors speak as if every tenderness has a price. Dialogue should carry elegance, threat, and hunger beneath courtesy.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Li Bihua
Break speech where longing meets pride. A name, accusation, or promise may stop because saying it completes a curse. Use repetition, delayed answer, and sudden coldness to show desire losing its mask.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Li Bihua
Make beauty physical and dangerous: a sleeve, cigarette, lipstick mark, wet hair, jewelry, doorway, bowl, blade, or mirror. Movement should reveal possession, envy, hunger, shame, or the pull of the dead.

{{// @category Influence }}
{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Cormac McCarthy voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. McCarthy's stark, violent, Biblical prose. No quotation marks, sparse punctuation, poetic brutality. Perfect for dark westerns, post-apocalyptic. }}
{{// @color #E67E22 }}
{{// @icon ✒️ }}
{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}

{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Cormac McCarthy}}

♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Cormac McCarthy
Use biblical parataxis and hard landscape. Concrete nouns, stark verbs, dust, blood, stone, iron, sky, hunger, and silence carry the weight. Sentences can run with terrible momentum or stop like a struck match. Keep judgment inside image and action.

♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Cormac McCarthy
Give speakers sparse, weathered speech. People state facts, bargains, warnings, and old beliefs with minimal explanation. Formal or archaic turns can appear when fate, violence, or law enters the room. Eloquence should feel earned by severity.

♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Cormac McCarthy
Use omission, hard stops, and plain refusal. Characters leave motives unsaid because survival, pride, or doom has made explanation useless. Repetition works as ritual or threat.

♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Cormac McCarthy
Use labor, travel, hunger, weapons, horses, fire, wounds, and distance. Physical beats should show endurance, violence, ritual, or judgment. The land presses on every body in the scene.

